# An application Kconfig becomes the Kconfig ROOT, so it has to source
# Kconfig.zephyr itself - and that source line must come LAST, after the
# options below, or they are not visible to a -DCONFIG_... on the command
# line.

menu "Portenta Machine Control Explorer"

config PMC_OPEN_PA1_ANALOG_SWITCH
	bool "Open the PA1 analog switch, freeing AI2 from the RMII clock"
	default y
	help
	  AI2 and the Ethernet RMII 50 MHz reference clock share the PA1/PA1_C
	  pad pair on the STM32H747, joined by a single SYSCFG analog switch.
	  Opening it separates them, which is what AI2 needs in order to read
	  anything meaningful rather than a 50 MHz clock.

	  Set this to n to leave that switch CLOSED. The only reason to do so
	  is to test whether the Ethernet MAC needs it closed to receive its
	  reference clock - with it closed, AI2 is measuring the clock and its
	  readings are meaningless. PC2 and PC3 are unaffected either way, so
	  AI0 and AI1 keep working regardless.

	  This is a genuine either/or if it turns out the clock arrives on the
	  PA1_C ball: one pad pair cannot serve an analog input and a 50 MHz
	  clock at the same time. See docs/MEASURED.md.

config PMC_ENCODER_CHANNELS
	int "How many encoder inputs to arm (0, 1 or 2)"
	default 2
	range 0 2
	help
	  A COUNT RATHER THAN A SWITCH, because it is the bisection tool.

	  Arming both channels stopped the WiFi radio answering any SDIO
	  IOCTL - measured, not suspected: with this at 0 the radio moved
	  1967 packets, with it at 2 every ioctl failed to get a buffer.
	  CAN was ruled out in the same cycle.

	  0 arms none. 1 arms ENC0 only (PJ8, PH12, PH11). 2 adds ENC1
	  (PC13, PI7, PJ10), and PC13 is the pin under suspicion: it sits on
	  the same port as SDMMC1's PC8..PC12, which is the bus the radio is
	  on, and it is the one encoder line with no alternate function at
	  all on this package.

config PMC_CANBUS
	bool "Bring up CAN on FDCAN1"
	default y
	help
	  FDCAN1 on PB8/PH13, and the transceiver's standby pin on PA13.

	  The other half of the same bisection as PMC_ENCODERS. PA13 is also
	  SWDIO, so this is the switch that gives a debugger back as well.

config PMC_CANOPEN
	bool "A minimal CANopen node on the CAN bus"
	depends on PMC_CANBUS
	default y
	help
	  Boot-up, producer heartbeat, the four NMT commands and an expedited
	  SDO server over five mandatory objects - enough that candump,
	  cansend and python-canopen see a real node rather than a controller
	  that can be made to send bytes.

	  NOT a CANopen stack. No PDOs, no segmented transfer, no SYNC, no
	  EMCY, no heartbeat consumer, no device profile. See app/src/canopen.h
	  for the full list of what is missing and why that is the honest
	  stopping point for a bring-up tool.

	  It costs one thread, and it transmits: with the heartbeat on and no
	  second node on the wire, the controller sits in ERROR_PASSIVE
	  retransmitting, which is what a lone CAN node does. Turn this off to
	  keep the bus silent, or set the heartbeat to 0 at runtime.

config PMC_CANOPEN_NODE_ID
	int "CANopen node id (1-127)"
	depends on PMC_CANOPEN
	default 5
	range 1 127
	help
	  Arbitrary, and changeable at runtime with `canopen node <n>`. It is
	  NOT remembered across a reset, deliberately: `canopen selftest`
	  asserts against a known identity, and a node id somebody has to look
	  up before trusting the output is worse than one that is always the
	  same at boot.

config PMC_CANOPEN_HEARTBEAT_MS
	int "Producer heartbeat period in ms (0 disables)"
	depends on PMC_CANOPEN
	default 1000
	range 0 65535
	help
	  1000 ms is the usual industrial default and is what a scanner
	  expects to wait for. 0 means the node says nothing until it is
	  asked something, which also means a scanner will not find it.

	  This is object 0x1017, so a CANopen master can write it over SDO,
	  and a reset puts it back to this value - which is what reset
	  communication is specified to do.

config PMC_ETH_PHY_RELATCH
	bool "Pulse the Ethernet PHY's reset again before the MAC initializes"
	default y
	help
	  The LAN8742A latches its configuration straps wrong at boot - mode
	  000 instead of 111, and nINTSEL selecting nINT rather than REFCLKO,
	  so the PHY sources no RMII reference clock and the MAC's DMA reset
	  never completes. The part re-samples those straps on every rising
	  edge of nRST, so pulsing it once more fixes it. See net.c and
	  docs/MEASURED.md.

	  Set this to n ONLY to reproduce the original fault, which is what it
	  exists for: the fix works, and the reason the first latch is wrong
	  is not yet known. An experiment that raises the PHY reset
	  regulator's startup-delay-us needs this off, or the pulse hides
	  whatever the delay did.

	  With this off and no other change, Ethernet does not come up. The
	  board is still reachable over WiFi and the health gate still
	  passes, so a build with it off is safe to flash.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
